## The Numbers Don't Lie
The food recall landscape this year has been nothing short of alarming. With a total of 419 recalls already, including 103 categorized as Class I (the most severe), it's clear that the food industry is facing significant quality control challenges. A shocking 74 of these recalls are tied to repeat offenders. While some brands are taking steps to address issues, others seem to be stuck in an unending loop of mistakes.

## May's Missteps: Leading Offenders
Recent recalls highlight troubling patterns. Renowned brands like `Silver Moon LP dba Loard's Ice Cream` and `California Dairies Inc.` feature prominently in this month's list with issues ranging from undeclared allergens like milk and soy to contamination by Salmonella. One particularly concerning recall involved `Raw Farm LLC`, facing scrutiny over an E. coli outbreak linked to their RAW FARM RAW CHEDDAR cheese.

## Worst Performers of the Year
But it's not just the recent recalls that are raising eyebrows. The data reveals concerning records for companies like `Moonlight Packing Corporation` (11 recalls, all Class I) and `Albertsons Companies LLC` (10 Class I recalls this year). These numbers place them at the top of our "Worst Performers" list for 2026.

## The Silver Moon Saga
`Silver Moon LP dba Loard's Ice Cream` alone accounts for a staggering number of Class II recalls this month, due to undeclared allergens in products like Loard's Peppermint Ice Cream and Loard's Champagne Sherbert. In a market where transparency and safety are paramount, this oversight is more than a minor hiccup--it's a breach of trust.

## Verdict: A Time for Change
The recurring nature of these issues underscores a pressing need for stricter monitoring and better compliance measures within the food industry. While it's easy to point fingers, it's more constructive to demand accountability and systemic change. After all, every recall affects not just the companies involved but also the consumers who trust them.

Stay tuned for next week's digest as we continue to scrutinize the numbers and hold brands accountable.
